WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.093
Security work starts with mapping the MCP environment.

00:00:03.093 --> 00:00:08.331
The diagram should show the AI host application, MCP client, MCP servers, tools, resources,

00:00:08.331 --> 00:00:12.073
prompt templates, user identity, service identity, downstream APIs, local files,

00:00:12.073 --> 00:00:13.944
cloud services, secrets, and tokens.

00:00:13.944 --> 00:00:18.434
It should distinguish local servers from remote servers because local execution and

00:00:18.434 --> 00:00:21.053
remote service access create different risk profiles.

00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:25.542
A local server may touch files or processes on the user's machine,

00:00:25.542 --> 00:00:29.658
while a remote server may rely on network trust, tenant isolation,

00:00:29.658 --> 00:00:31.155
and external service controls.

00:00:31.155 --> 00:00:35.645
A useful architecture diagram shows data flow, authority flow, and action flow.

00:00:35.645 --> 00:00:40.509
Data flow answers what information enters the model context, tool arguments, logs, resources,

00:00:40.509 --> 00:00:41.631
and downstream services.

00:00:41.631 --> 00:00:46.495
Authority flow answers which identity or token is used when a tool acts.

00:00:46.495 --> 00:00:51.733
Action flow answers what can be read, written, sent, created, deleted, approved, or changed.

00:00:51.733 --> 00:00:54.352
These flows do not always line up.

00:00:54.352 --> 00:00:58.842
A user may ask a question, the model may propose an action,

00:00:58.842 --> 00:01:04.829
and a tool may execute under a service account with broader permissions than the user expected.

00:01:04.829 --> 00:01:09.318
Trust boundaries appear between user input, model output, tool metadata, tool execution,

00:01:09.318 --> 00:01:11.189
external content, and downstream systems.

00:01:11.189 --> 00:01:13.434
User input is not automatically safe.

00:01:13.434 --> 00:01:15.679
Model output is not automatically authorized.

00:01:15.679 --> 00:01:20.917
Tool descriptions are not neutral; they influence how the model thinks about tool use.

00:01:20.917 --> 00:01:24.285
External resources may contain stale, misleading, or hostile instructions.

00:01:24.285 --> 00:01:26.904
Downstream systems may enforce their own policies,

00:01:26.904 --> 00:01:32.142
but the MCP application still decides what to request and what context to expose.

00:01:32.142 --> 00:01:34.387
Architecture visibility prevents vague risk discussions.

00:01:34.387 --> 00:01:37.380
Instead of saying an agent can use tools,

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:42.618
the team can say which host connects to which servers, which tools are available,

00:01:42.618 --> 00:01:47.108
which resources are exposed, which credentials are used, and where approvals happen.

00:01:47.108 --> 00:01:50.850
That clarity supports least privilege, monitoring, incident response, and governance.

00:01:50.850 --> 00:01:55.339
Without it, teams end up trusting the model, the tool, the server,

00:01:55.339 --> 00:01:58.333
and the user interface as one blended boundary,

00:01:58.333 --> 00:02:01.700
which is exactly what MCP security needs to avoid.
